  "prediction": "The Department of Defense (DOD) establishes foreign currency budget rates to determine its funding needs for overseas expenditures. These rates are used to calculate gains or losses due to foreign currency fluctuations. The report examines DOD's methodology for establishing foreign currency budget rates and its management of the Foreign Currency Fluctuations Defense (FCFD) account.\n\nDOD has used various methodologies to establish foreign currency budget rates, including a centered weighted average model and a 6-month average of Wall Street Journal rates. The report found that DOD's revised budget rates in fiscal years 2014-2016 were more closely aligned with Treasury rates, resulting in decreased projected O&M and MILPERS funding needs.\n\nHowever, the report also found that DOD does not effectively manage the FCFD account balance based on projected losses. Since fiscal year 2012, DOD has maintained the FCFD account balance at the maximum level of $970 million, without considering projected losses. This may result in maintaining balances that are hundreds of millions of dollars higher than needed.\n\nThe report also found that DOD's financial reporting on foreign currency fluctuations is incomplete and inaccurate, hindering the quality of information available to manage the FCFD account. The Army's financial reporting is particularly inaccurate due to its accounting system's failure to record disbursements consistent with DOD's Financial Management Regulation.\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to DOD:\n\n1. Conduct a review of foreign currency rates used at disbursement to determine cost-saving opportunities.\n2. Analyze realized and projected losses to determine the necessary size of the FCFD account balance.\n3. Revise the Financial Management Regulation to include guidance on ensuring complete and accurate data in foreign currency reports.\n4. Develop a plan with timelines for implementing changes to the Army's General Fund Enterprise Business System to accurately record disbursements.\n\nDOD concurred with the first, third, and fourth recommendations, but partially concurred with the second recommendation, citing the difficulty in forecasting foreign currency rates due to market volatility.",
